        "original_id": "3b6c90b1-6c4c-5305-870d-5c49763375b3",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Isaak Dunayevsky, Grigori Aleksandrov\n- Roles: Soviet film composer, conductor, film director\n- Topics: Soviet film, operetta, film comedies, honors\n\nFacts:\n- Isaak Osipovich Dunayevsky was born on 30 January 1900 and died on 25 July 1955.\n- Grigori Vasilyevich Aleksandrov was born on 23 January 1903 and died on 16 December 1983.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky was a Soviet film composer and conductor active in the 1930s and 1940s.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky achieved major success in music for operetta and film comedies.\n- Isaak Dunayevsky frequently worked with the Soviet film director Grigori Aleksandrov.\n- Grigori Aleksandrov was a prominent Soviet film director whose original family name was Mormonenko.\n- Grigori Aleksandrov was named a People's Artist of the USSR in 1947.\n- Grigori Aleksandrov was named a Hero of Socialist Labor in 1973.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9ca473b9-71db-505f-85de-4b0918265477",
        "text": "Page: Isaak Dunayevsky. Sentence: Isaak Osipovich Dunayevsky (Russian: \"Исаак Осипович Дунаевский\" ; also transliterated as Dunaevski or Dunaevsky; 30 January [O.S. 18 January] 1900 25 July 1955) was a Soviet film composer and conductor of the 1930s and 1940s, who achieved huge success in music for operetta and film comedies, frequently working with the film director Grigori Aleksandrov.\nPage: Grigori Aleksandrov. Sentence: Grigori Vasilyevich Aleksandrov or Alexandrov (Russian: Григо́рий Васи́льевич Алекса́ндров ; original family name was Мормоненко or Mormonenko; 23 January 1903 – 16 December 1983) was a prominent Soviet film director who was named a People's Artist of the USSR in 1947 and a Hero of Socialist Labor in 1973.",
